#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79f1049a-8af9-50ee-9dac-9dbd9c19bdc6" :knowledge-point/title "Correctly Identifying a Scatter Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "b4051787-5d60-53bd-b390-35585ff6ef32" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9679" :question/problem "Johnny wants to find out if there is a relationship between a person's height and their weight. So he records the heights and corresponding weights of some of his classmates. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n| **Height (cm)** | **$165$** | **$170$** | **$170$** | **$175$** | **$180$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $60$ | $70$ | $75$ | $70$ | $75$ |\n\nConstruct a scatter plot that represents this data." :question/worked-solution "We create a scatter diagram in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- First, we draw the independent variable (height) along the horizontal axis.\n- Then, we draw the dependent variable (weight) along the vertical axis.\n- Finally, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data.\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/630817a152951fe1e8bafba13db730ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "35dd0c47-2ee8-57bf-b6c6-65af8ac3c76d" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9680" :question/problem "The scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of bags of popcorn sold one day and the price per bag at nine locations.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/1db6081814f3b5c2b488e394c39648bb.png)\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Only one location sold exactly $10$ bags of popcorn.\n2. Two locations sold bags of popcorn for $\\[math]1.40$ each.\n3. No location sold more than $30$ bags of popcorn." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10$, namely, $(1.8,10)$. This means only one location sold exactly $10$ bags of popcorn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/bade3d9c0d8c1087bd40c530f25d7a86.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $1.4$, namely, $(1.4,30)$. This means that only one location sold popcorn for $\\[math]1.40$ per bag.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/b4c4042789d2ef6a2abe2a17590347c3.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one location sold more than $30$ bags of popcorn, represented by the dot above the horizontal line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/d4ecff9d77bdc687ca7c3ccb754dc58a.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f99f2aa-f977-5d6e-ae62-a826a897ba9b" :knowledge-point/title "Reading a Scatter Plot"}] :question/id #uuid "0eb6bb49-3b2c-5709-acc8-6a5f78998c89" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9682" :question/problem "A group of $10$ students is surveyed. The scatter plot above shows the relationship between a student's number of hours of sleep per night and their overall grade point average (GPA). How many hours of sleep per night does the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ get?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/a43e082da00d114b014f5b28a8e0bb21.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/f5f13db695eeca6df5ed4ebc336a86f7.png)\n\nTo determine the number of hours of sleep the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ gets, we locate $80$ on the vertical axis and move in the horizontal direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(6,80)$. Therefore, the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ sleeps for $6$ hours per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3cf74b6-ea62-5325-bbbf-2248a4b7d5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148945} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd79eea-569b-5697-9417-2ce1dbe46ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148946} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed4260a-7d67-5699-be7e-308b43af2093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148947} {:answer/id #uuid "38304cfe-fc2f-5846-bfc5-de27186897e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148948} {:answer/id #uuid "73eec4d7-ee5f-50a4-88eb-cbc108270aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38304cfe-fc2f-5846-bfc5-de27186897e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148948} :answer-field/id #uuid "b07095f1-cf57-5e8c-a40e-330019508c78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148944}] :question/id #uuid "1109475d-9e0b-5521-b99e-3e1bd3fbb649" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-133622" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/q-133622.png)\n\nSarah interviewed some parents, asking them how much time their children spend playing outdoors per day. The information she received is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. There is only one child who spends $3.5$ hours playing outdoors.\n2. There is only one child aged $6$ among those surveyed.\n3. None of the children spend less than $2$ hours playing outdoors."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9efe8a2-af82-54db-bef2-47217eb1d85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148999} {:answer/id #uuid "ea63764c-ad41-5a00-a5e2-128d4f53cb3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149000} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5be3cc-2571-5824-966a-18fe74eac1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149001} {:answer/id #uuid "21d1a35f-f429-58bb-9cdb-6649f3eab00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149002} {:answer/id #uuid "7a430968-e1ca-5f54-aaac-8b32bb26a033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a430968-e1ca-5f54-aaac-8b32bb26a033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149003} :answer-field/id #uuid "45871b81-94e6-596b-8f2a-6e55eb94474c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148998}] :question/id #uuid "35f73958-c27b-5887-a2cc-b747d3896473" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-134133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2585/Source/Images/q-134133.png)\n\nMr. Clark recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above.\n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. Only one player is $195cm$ tall.\n3. There are exactly two players taller than $185cm$."}]}
